## Relationship to Rules

### How Rules Influence Agent Reasoning

Rules act as constraints and guidance for agent decision-making:

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A[Agent receives prompt] --> B[Load applicable rules]
    B --> C{Rule matches context?}
    C -->|No rules| D[Agent uses default reasoning]
    C -->|Rules match| E[Apply rule constraints]
    
    E --> F{Any constraints?}
    F -->|Yes| G[Reasoning guided by rules]
    F -->|No constraints| H[Agent uses default reasoning]
    
    G --> I[Execute with rule guidance]
    H --> I
    D --> I
```

### Rule Influence Examples

| Rule Type | Agent Behavior Impact |
|-----------|---------------------|
| **Always-on rules** (`alwaysApply: true`) | Always present in context, guide all decisions |
| **Auto-attached rules** (`globs: ["*.ts"]`) | Loaded when matching files are in context |
| **Agent-requested rules** (`description: "..."`) | Agent decides when to fetch and apply |
| **Manual rules** (`@rule-name`) | Explicitly invoked by user |

### Rules as Reasoning Anchors

```markdown
Rule: "Never hardcode API keys"

Agent reasoning with rule:
  [Context] User wants to add database connection
  [Check rules] Rule says: never hardcode keys
  [Plan] Use process.env.DB_URL instead of literal string
  [Execute] Generate: const dbUrl = process.env.DB_URL
```

